# QUESTION : STALE DATA KYA HOTA HAI ?
========================================

Cache use karne ka ek important problem hai — Database aur Cache ke beech data inconsistent ho sakta hai.

## EXAMPLE :
-------------

Step 1: Pehli baar GET /products/101 aayi → Cache Miss → Application ne DB se data fetch karke Cache me store kiya:
        Database / Cache:  product:101  →  price = ₹79,999

Step 2: Ab product ka price update hua:

       PUT /products/101

       Database:  product:101  →  price = ₹69,999
       Cache:     product:101  →  price = ₹79,999  (purana data)


Step 3: Ab next request GET /products/101 pe Cache Hit hoga, aur user ko purana price ₹79,999 milega.
        Is purane/outdated cached data ko Stale Data kehte hain.




================================================
# QUESTION : CACHE INVALIDATION KYA HOTA HAI ?
================================================

- Database update hone par Cache ke outdated data ko delete ya update karna,
  taaki application stale data serve na kare. Iske 2 approaches hain:

1. DELETE / INVALIDATE CACHE
-----------------------------
- DB update → Corresponding cache key (`product:101`) ko delete kar do (`DEL product:101`).
- Next read for the same key → Cache Miss → DB se latest data fetch → Cache repopulate.

2. UPDATE CACHE
----------------
- DB update → Corresponding cache key (`product:101`) ki existing value ko latest data se replace kar do.



--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: AGAR CACHE INVALIDATION STALE DATA KO PREVENT KARTI HAI, TO TTL KI ZARURAT KYUN HOTI HAI?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- TTL (Time To Live) ek automatic expiration mechanism hai jo cache entries ko specified time ke baad expire kar deta hai. 

## 1. CACHE INVALIDATION CAN FAIL
-----------------------------------

- DB update ke baad application Redis ko call karti hai cache invalidate karne ke liye. 
  Lekin Redis node down/slow hone, network failure, request timeout ya application crash
  ki wajah se invalidation fail ho sakti hai.

- Aise mein DB mein latest data (`price = ₹69,999`) update ho jaata hai, lekin Cache mein 
  old data (`price = ₹79,999`) reh jaata hai. Agar TTL configured nahi hai, toh stale entry
  indefinitely Cache mein reh sakti hai, jab tak koi subsequent operation use update ya delete na kare.

- TTL = 10 minutes hone par entry expire ho jaayegi. Next read par Cache Miss hoga, aur application
  DB se latest data fetch kar sakegi.

- TTL Cache Invalidation ka replacement nahi hai. Ye ek safety mechanism hai jo invalidation fail hone par 
  stale data ko indefinitely Cache mein rehne se rokta hai.


## 2. DATA MAY CHANGE WITHOUT THIS APPLICATION KNOWING
------------------------------------------------------

- Maan lo Product Service mein DB update/delete ke baad Cache invalidate karne ka logic hai. 
  Lekin agar koi doosri service (e.g., Order Service) directly Product DB mein data update/delete 
  kar de aur Cache invalidate na kare, toh Cache mein stale data reh sakta hai.

- Aise case mein TTL ek safety net provide karta hai. TTL expire hone tak Cache se stale data mil sakta hai. 
  Lekin TTL expire hone ke baad next request par Cache Miss hoga, application DB se latest data fetch karke 
  Cache repopulate karegi, aur subsequent requests par client ko fresh data milega.



## 3. MEMORY MANAGEMENT
-------------------------

Cache RAM use karta hai, aur RAM limited hoti hai.

- Product Service continuously new products cache karti hai, lekin kuch entries future mein kabhi access nahi hoti, 
  jisse woh unnecessarily RAM occupy karti rehti hain.
- TTL aisi entries ko automatically expire karne mein help karta hai, taaki woh indefinitely RAM occupy na karein.


### IMPORTANT : REDIS RAM LIMIT vs TOTAL SERVER RAM
-----------------------------------------------------

Total Server RAM ≠ Redis ki available RAM

Suppose server ki Total RAM = 32 GB, jisme OS, Application, Database,
aur Redis sab RAM share karte hain.

Redis ke liye ek configured limit hoti hai:

Redis Max Memory = 8 GB

Jab Redis apni configured max memory (max allocated RAM for Redis) limit tak pahunch jaata hai,
aur naya data store karna hota hai, to Redis ki configured eviction policy decide karti hai ki 
existing entries me se kaunsi entry remove ki jaaye RAM se, taaki naye data ke liye space ban sake.




=============================================
# QUESTION : CACHE EVICTION KYA HOTA HAI ?
=============================================

Cache ki configured RAM limit reach hone par, eviction policy ke according existing cache entries ko
RAM se remove karna, taaki new data store karne ke liye RAM mein space available ho sake.

---------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION : CACHE EVICTION KI ZARURAT KYUN PADTI HAI ? 
---------------------------------------------------------

- Suppose Service rapidly new entries Cache mein store kar rahi hai. Har entry ka TTL hai, lekin existing entries
  ka TTL expire hokar RAM free hone se pehle hi new entries aati rehti hain, jisse Cache ki configured RAM limit
  reach ho sakti hai.

- Aise scenario mein Eviction Policy ke according existing cache entries remove ki jaati hain, taaki new entries
  store karne ke liye RAM available ho sake.


------------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION : GENERIC CACHE EVICTION STRATEGIES KONSI HAI ? 
------------------------------------------------------------

1. LRU — Least Recently Used
------------------------------

LRU me woh cache entry remove ki jaati hai
jo sabse zyada time se access nahi hui hai.

Example:

product:101 → Last Accessed: 1 minute ago
product:102 → Last Accessed: 5 minutes ago
product:103 → Last Accessed: 30 minutes ago

Memory full hone par agar `product:104` store karna ho,
to LRU policy `product:103` ko evict kar sakti hai.


2. LFU — Least Frequently Used
--------------------------------

LFU me woh cache entry remove ki jaati hai
jise sabse kam number of times access kiya gaya hai.

Example:

product:101 → Access Count = 1000
product:102 → Access Count = 500
product:103 → Access Count = 2

Memory full hone par agar `product:104` store karna ho,
to LFU policy `product:103` ko evict kar sakti hai.


3. FIFO — First In First Out
------------------------------

Jab Cache ki RAM limit reach ho jaati hai aur nayi entry store karni hoti hai, to sabhi available cache
entries me se jo entry sabse pehle (oldest) Cache me insert hui thi, use remove kiya jaata hai.

Example:

Insertion Order:  A → B → C

Memory full hone par `D` insert karne ke liye:

A → Evicted

Result:  B → C → D


4. RANDOM EVICTION
--------------------

Is approach me Cache randomly kisi existing
cache entry ko remove kar sakta hai.



------------------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION : REDIS SPECIFIC CACHE EVICTION STRATEGIES KYA HAI ? 
------------------------------------------------------------------

Redis generic cache eviction policies ko apne specific eviction policies ke through implement karta hai. 
Jab Redis configured maxmemory limit reach karta hai, to selected eviction policy ke according keys evict 
ki ja sakti hain.

  
                          REDIS CACHE EVICTION POLICIES
                                         │
          ┌──────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┐
          │                              │                              │
      ALLKEYS                        VOLATILE                      noeviction
   (Saari keys eligible)      (Sirf TTL wali keys eligible)
          │                              │
   ┌──────┼──────┐                 ┌──────┼──────┬─────────┐
   │      │      │                 │      │      │         │
  LRU    LFU   RANDOM             LRU    LFU   RANDOM     TTL
   │      │      │                 │      │      │         │
allkeys- allkeys- allkeys-    volatile- volatile- volatile- volatile-
  lru     lfu    random          lru      lfu     random     ttl
 

### NOTE : FIFO → Redis directly support nahi karta.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION : KAUNSI REDIS KEYS CACHE EVICTION KE LIYE ELIGIBLE HAIN?
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

Redis keys ko do groups me consider karta hai:

a) allkeys
   → Redis ki saari keys eviction ke liye eligible hain,
     chahe unke saath TTL configured ho ya nahi.

b) volatile
   → Sirf woh keys eviction ke liye eligible hain
     jin par TTL configured hai.

Ab configured eviction algorithm decide karega
ki in eligible keys me se kaunsi key remove karni hai.


-------------------------------------------------------------------
### QUESTION: REDIS ME KAUNSE CACHE EVICTION ALGORITHMS HOTE HAIN?
-------------------------------------------------------------------

1. noeviction — Configured maxmemory limit reach hone pe koi eviction nahi hoti. 
                Incoming Redis write operation reject kar di jaati hai aur error 
                return hota hai.
2. allkeys-lru — Saari keys me se woh key remove ki jaati hai jo sabse zyada time se access nahi hui.
3. allkeys-lfu — Saari keys me se woh key remove ki jaati hai jise sabse kam number of times access kiya gaya.
4. allkeys-random — Saari keys me se randomly koi ek key remove ki jaati hai.
5. volatile-lru — Sirf TTL wali keys me se woh key remove ki jaati hai jo sabse zyada time se access nahi hui.
6. volatile-lfu — Sirf TTL wali keys me se woh key remove ki jaati hai jise sabse kam number of times access kiya gaya.
7. volatile-random — Sirf TTL wali keys me se randomly koi ek key remove ki jaati hai.
8. volatile-ttl — Sirf TTL wali keys me se wo key evict ki jaati hai jiski remaining TTL sabse kam hai (jo sabse jaldi expire hone wali hai).


### IMPORTANT
---------------

Cache eviction ke liye key eligibility aur eviction algorithm — dono Redis ki global configuration me define hote hain,
maxmemory-policy property ke through. Ye kisi individual key pe define nahi hoti.

maxmemory-policy allkeys-lru      → Saari keys eligible, LRU algo. se evict
maxmemory-policy allkeys-lfu      → Saari keys eligible, LFU algo. se evict
maxmemory-policy allkeys-random   → Saari keys eligible, random algo. se evict
maxmemory-policy volatile-lru     → Sirf TTL wali keys eligible, LRU algo. se evict
maxmemory-policy volatile-lfu     → Sirf TTL wali keys eligible, LFU algo. se evict
maxmemory-policy volatile-random  → Sirf TTL wali keys eligible, random algo. se evict
maxmemory-policy volatile-ttl     → Sirf TTL wali keys eligible, shortest remaining TTL se evict
maxmemory-policy noeviction       → Koi eviction nahi, write reject


### NOTE :
-----------
Volatile policies me sirf TTL wali keys eviction candidates hoti hain. Agar memory full hone par
sufficient eligible TTL keys available nahi hain, to Redis required memory free nahi kar paayega
aur write operation fail ho sakta hai.




==================================================
# QUESTION: CACHE WRITE PATTERNS KYA HOTE HAIN?
==================================================

Abhi tak humne mainly read path dekha (jaise Cache-Aside pattern). Lekin real problem write ke time aati hai
jab data update hota hai, to Database aur Cache ko kis order me update karein? Isi problem ko handle karne ke
liye different Cache Write Patterns use kiye jaate hain.

-------------------------
1. WRITE-THROUGH CACHE
-------------------------

- Write-Through me Database update ke saath-saath corresponding Cache key ke
  against data ko bhi latest value se update kar diya jaata hai.
  Yahan corresponding Cache entry ko invalidate (i.e. delete) nahi karte 
  usi existing cache key ke against nayi value se update karte hain.

## EXAMPLE :
-------------

   Cache me:     product:101  →  price = ₹79,999
   Update aaya:  PUT /products/101 (new price = ₹69,999)

   Write-Through:
    → DB update (price = ₹69,999)
    → Cache update (product:101 = ₹69,999)   ← same key, nayi value


## QUESTION: WRITE-THROUGH CACHE KE BENIFITS ? 
------------------------------------------------
- Next read request pe Cache Hit → latest data directly Cache se return.


## QUESTION: WRITE-THROUGH CACHE KA USE-CASE ?
------------------------------------------------
Write-Through useful ho sakta hai jab updated data ko
immediately aur frequently read kiya jana expected ho,
kyunki updated data pehle se Cache me available hota hai.


## QUESTION: WRITE-THROUGH CACHE KI LIMITATIONS ? 
---------------------------------------------------

Write-Through me Database aur Cache dono ko update karna hota hai, 
isliye failure scenarios handle karne padte hain.


### CASE 1: DATABASE UPDATE SUCCESS, CACHE UPDATE FAILS
--------------------------------------------------------

Database successfully update ho gaya, lekin Cache update fail ho gaya. Is wajah se Cache abhi bhi purana 
data return karega — matlab stale data temporarily serve hoga. Lekin agar Cache entry ke saath TTL configured hai,
to TTL expire hone ke baad next request Database se latest data fetch karke Cache ko update kar degi aur stale data 
automatically refresh ho jaayega.


### CASE 2: CACHE UPDATE SUCCESS, DATABASE UPDATE FAILS
--------------------------------------------------------

Cache me naya data store ho gaya, lekin Database update fail ho gaya. Isliye initially, 
jab tak Cache ka TTL expire nahi hua hai, tab tak temporarily users ko updated data mil 
sakta hai. Lekin TTL expire hone ke baad subsequent requests par Cache Miss hoga, aur 
Database se purana data fetch hoke Cache me store ho jaayega — jisse subsequent requests
me users ko old data milna shuru ho jaayega. Matlab jo updated data Cache me temporarily 
tha, wo Database me persist nahi hua → eventually lost.


#### IMPORTANT :
----------------
Practical systems me Cache-Aside (read flow) + Cache Invalidation ko commonly use karte hain  kyunki ye
implement aur maintain karne me simple hai, aur commonly preferred approach hai. Database source of truth
rehta hai aur Cache ko temporary data ke roop me treat kiya jaata hai.



-------------------------------------
2. WRITE-BEHIND / WRITE-BACK CACHE
-------------------------------------

- Write-Through me Database update ke saath-saath Cache bhi latest data se update kiya jaata hai.
- Lekin Write-Behind me approach different hai: Jab data update karne ki request aati hai, to 
  Application pehle updated data ko Cache me store karti hai aur client ko turant success response
  return kar deti hai. Uske baad Database me data ko asynchronously update kiya jaata hai.
  i.e Database ko immediately update nahi kiya jata.


## QUESTION: WRITE-BEHIND KE BENIFITS ? 
-----------------------------------------

Write-Behind ka main benefit ye hai ki client ko Database update complete hone ka wait nahi karna padta 
isliye client ko jaldi response milta hai, jisse response time aur user experience better ho jaata hai.
Ye high write workloads me useful hota hai. 


## QUESTION: WRITE-BEHIND KE LIMITATIONS ? 
-----------------------------------------

Write-Behind me client ko immediately success response mil jaata hai, jabki Database update asynchronously 
later hota hai. Agar Database update hone se pehle Cache fail ho jaye, to updated data Database me persist 
nahi hoga → data loss ka risk.




-------------------------
3. WRITE-AROUND CACHE
-------------------------

- Write-Through → Database update ke saath-saath Cache me bhi latest data update kiya jaata hai.
- Write-Behind / Write-Back → Updated data pehle Cache me store kiya jaata hai, Database update baad me asynchronously hota hai.
- Write-Around ka main idea ye hai ki write request ke case me new data directly Database me write/update kiya jaata hai, aur agar 
  corresponding data pehle se Cache me present hai, to stale data avoid karne ke liye us Cache entry ko delete kar diya jaata hai.
- Ab same data ke liye next read request par Cache Miss hoga. Is case me Application latest data Database se fetch karke Cache me 
  store karti hai aur response return kiya jaata hai.


## QUESTION: WRITE-AROUND KA USE-CASE ? 
-----------------------------------------

Write-Around Cache useful hota hai jab data frequently update hota ho, lekin frequently read nahi hota. Aise case me har write ke
baad Cache ko update karna unnecessary ho jaata hai. Isse Redis ki RAM bhi bachti hai — kyunki jo data baar-baar read nahi hota, 
usko cache me rakhne ka koi fayda nahi.


## QUESTION: WRITE-AROUND KE LIMITATIONS ? 
--------------------------------------------

Write ke time Cache entry delete kar di jaati hai, isliye next read par Cache Miss hoga aur Application ko data DB se fetch karna padega 
jo Cache se slower hai. Isliye write ke baad first read comparatively slower hoti hai.





==========================================
# QUESTION: CACHE STAMPEDE KYA HOTA HAI?
==========================================

Cache Stampede (ise Thundering Herd bhi kehte hain) tab hota hai jab koi popular cached data unavailable ho jaata hai, 
aur uske turant baad usi data ke liye bahut saari concurrent requests ek saath aa jaati hain. Ye saari requests Cache 
Miss encounter karti hain, aur same data fetch karne ke liye ek saath Database ko hit karne lagti hain.

Suppose ek popular product cache me stored hai:

Key:    product:101
Value:  { "id": 101, "name": "iPhone", "price": 79999 }
TTL:    10 minutes

TTL expire hone ke baad product:101 Cache se remove ho jaata hai. Exactly usi time usi product ke liye 10,000 concurrent 
requests aa jaati hain.  Sab ko Cache Miss milta hai, aur ab ye saari requests independently Database ko hit karne lagti hain.
Isse Database par sudden load spike → Database overload ho sakta hai → response time increase → overall application performance impact.



---------------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: CACHE STAMPEDE KE KYA-KYA REASONS HO SAKTE HAIN?
---------------------------------------------------------------

Popular cache entry unavailable hone ke common reasons:

1. TTL Expiry — Popular cache key ka TTL expire ho jaata hai.
2. Cache Eviction — Memory pressure ke kaaran eviction policy popular cache entry ko remove kar deti hai.
3. Cache Invalidation — Data update hone par popular cache entry delete kar di jaati hai.
4. Cache Restart / Failure — Cache server restart, fail, ya temporarily unavailable ho jaata hai.

In sab cases me, cache entry unavailable hone ke turant baad usi data ke liye bahut saari 
concurrent requests aa jaati hain → Cache Stampede.



----------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: CACHE STAMPEDE KE SOLUTIONS kYA HAI ?
----------------------------------------------------

1. LOCKING / REQUEST COALESCING  
---------------------------------

### CORE IDEA
---------------

Same cache key ke liye multiple concurrent Cache Misses hone par sab requests independently Database ko hit na karein.
Ideally, sirf ek request Database se data fetch karke Cache ko populate kare, aur baaki requests us result ka wait karein. 
Cache populate hone ke baad, woh requests Cache se data retrieve kar sakein.


1. SINGLE SERVICE INSTANCE — LOCAL LOCK
-------------------------------------------

- Agar Service ka sirf ek instance hai aur saari requests usi instance par aa rahi hain, to Cache Stampede
  handle karne ke liye local lock use kiya ja sakta hai (Java me synchronized block/method).

- Hum DB se data fetch karne wala logic ko Cache Miss ke case me ek synchronized method/block me likh dete hain.
  Ek time pe sirf ek thread synchronized block me execute karta hai; baaki threads wait karte hain.

- Jab Cache Stampede hota hai, to bahut saari requests (hence threads) Cache Miss encounter karti hain aur sabhi
  threads local lock acquire karne ki koshish karte hain, taaki DB se data fetch kar sakein. Lekin sirf ek thread
  hi local lock acquire kar pata hai.

- Jis thread ko synchronized block access karne ka mauka milta hai, wo DB se data fetch karta hai aur Cache populate
  karta hai. Uske baad wo thread local lock release kar deta hai. Ab baaki requests (jo wait kar rahi thi) me se kisi 
  ek ko synchronized block access karne ka mauka milta hai.

- Hum DB access karne se pehle ek aur baar check karte hain ki Redis me data exist karta hai ya nahi. Kyunki jo bahar
  wala Redis lookup tha, wo already saari concurrent requests ne pass kar liya tha (sabko Cache Miss mila tha). Ab pehli 
  request ne already Cache populate kar diya hai — to agar ye second Cache check na ho, to subsequent requests bhi DB call
  karne lagengi. Isliye DB access karne se pehle ek aur Redis lookup (second Cache check) zaroori hota hai.


## Conceptually:
------------------

// First Cache Check
data = redis.get(key);

if (data != null) {
    return data;
}

synchronized (lock) {
    // Check Cache Again
    data = redis.get(key);

    if (data != null) {
        return data;
    }

    // Fetch from Database
    data = database.get(key);

    // Store in Cache
    redis.set(key, data);

    return data;
}




